{
  "dom_id": "step-q115377",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2?$",
  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $8x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n8x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/efbc925cd1aca7f476d6cfc11f3c668c4f3bde78acf359eb0a65696ce2a12852.png)",
